1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,600
Computer vision & Robotics.

2
00:00:02,799 --> 00:00:05,799
A Tic-Tac-Toe system built under real-world constraints.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,800
Actuation error: gear backlash and limited repeatability create millimetre-scale pose errors that vision must correct.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,199
Illumination changes with daylight and lamps, so fixed colour thresholds are not photometrically invariant.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:16,600
The wrist camera is low grade: noisy, compressed and lens-distorted.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:21,600
Three-year-old deep detectors and segmenters were heavy, and they lowered the control rate on a laptop CPU.

7
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:26,399
Colour is classified in HSV, where brightness is separated from hue and saturation.

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,600
Each cell is a circular region: every pixel votes, which is more stable than averaging.

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:35,000
White-patch balancing and CLAHE reduce the effect of changing illumination.

10
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,600
The hue ranges were measured on the real chips under laboratory lighting.

11
00:00:40,399 --> 00:00:46,200
To detect chips in dim, over-exposed or specially lit rooms, where camera colours are unreliable, we chose the image subtraction method.

12
00:00:46,399 --> 00:00:52,399
Twice per second the robot photographs the board and subtracts consecutive images; what remains is the new or moved chip.

13
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:58,600
The centre of that region is added to the list of known chip positions, or it updates the position of a chip that has moved.

14
00:00:58,799 --> 00:01:05,000
Colours are not detected at all: the robot follows every chip from the start of the game and labels the opponent's chips itself.

15
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,599
Seat play of about 5 mm makes the difference image noisy, so the largest closed area and its centroid are used.

16
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,400
Calibration uses fiducial circles on the paper as references.

17
00:01:15,599 --> 00:01:20,599
Two points define a similarity transform in closed form: rotation, scale and translation.

18
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:25,400
The grid, chip trays and token spots follow the paper wherever it lies.

19
00:01:25,599 --> 00:01:29,599
A hand-eye step maps pixels to robot coordinates for a downward-looking camera.

20
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:35,599
Segmentation test on a laptop camera with a COCO-trained deep network.

21
00:01:35,799 --> 00:01:41,000
It labels chairs and animals but knows nothing about our chips, and needs a GPU for real time.

22
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,200
The classical alternative: HSV masks, distance transform and watershed give one mask per chip.

23
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:49,599
About 60 ms per frame on a single CPU core, even when chips touch.

24
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,400
Depth estimation: a network such as Depth Anything V2 gives relative depth, shown as a heat map beside the original image.

25
00:01:55,599 --> 00:02:00,599
For metric distance the chip diameter is the ruler: Z equals focal length times diameter divided by the apparent diameter.

26
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:05,000
To avoid hitting chips, the board or the other arm, the robot keeps a safe clearance; it is measured only in the first passes.

27
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:07,599
Then the model has learned the distances and moves without re-measuring.

28
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:14,000
Paths are learned: the robot tries lift heights on simulated transfers and keeps the shortest collision-free one.

29
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:19,400
A regression then predicts the lift from distance and obstacle height for every new transfer.

30
00:02:19,599 --> 00:02:26,000
In 3-D the arc is parabolic in height and can bend sideways; the tool descends vertically onto the chip.

31
00:02:26,199 --> 00:02:32,000
It picks a chip from its tray, places it on the target cell and then moves to the red chip.

32
00:02:32,199 --> 00:02:38,500
If an obstacle suddenly blocks the path, a lateral detour is searched and the move continues.

33
00:02:38,699 --> 00:02:43,599
The same idea applies to grasp-success prediction, anomaly detection for moved chips and per-lamp colour models.

34
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,400
In dim light detection becomes unreliable, so positions were taught by hand.

35
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:55,599
The arm is guided in learning mode, each pose is read and stored, and the code replays the table.

36
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:01,800
To avoid collisions, the waiting robot folds into a rest pose that the other arm cannot reach.

37
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,599
Its camera watches only the red token region and starts when the token appears.

38
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,599
A fixed observation point gives every board reading the same viewpoint.

39
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,800
Decision: alpha-beta minimax never loses and prefers faster wins.

40
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,599
A move is accepted only after the vision output is verified against the expected board.

41
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:24,000
Everything was first validated in a SolidWorks motion study.

42
00:03:24,199 --> 00:03:27,599
Then the board was designed, built from wood and painted.

43
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:32,000
Hadi Sarhangi Fard, AI Engineer / Mechanical Engineer.

44
00:03:32,199 --> 00:03:35,599
